==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{root|la|ine-pro|*(s)tewp-}}
From {{inh|la|ine-pro||*(s)tup-éh₁-ye-ti|lit=to be struck}}, from {{af|ine-pro|*(s)tewp-|t1=to push, hit|*-éh₁yeti|pos2=stative verb suffix|nocat=1}}.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|stupeō|593}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|stupeō}}

===Verb===
{{la-verb|2.nopass|stupeō|stupu}}

# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to be [[stunned]], [[stiffened]] or [[benumbed]], [[stop]], [[hesitate]]
# {{lb|la|intransitive}} to be [[dazed]], [[speechless]] or [[silenced]]; to be [[astounded]], [[confounded]], [[aghast]] or [[amazed]]
#* {{Q|la|Seneca the Younger|Phaedra|607|quote=Curae leves loquuntur, ingentes '''stupent'''.|t=Trivial concerns talk, great ones are '''speechless'''.}}
# {{lb|la|transitive}} to be [[astonished]] or [[amazed]] at, [[wonder]] at
#: {{syn|la|astupeō|admīror|mīror|attonō|obstupēscō|stupēscō|dēfīgō}}

====Conjugation====
{{la-conj|2.nopass|stupeō|stupu}}

====Usage Notes====
To convey the phrase of "to surprise," the subject and object have to be reversed, due to there being a lack of passive forms.

